    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d199">

                <pb facs="0406" n="392" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg392"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EAC/11–944: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in the
                        United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gallman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-11T12:00:00-04:00">November 11,
                            1944—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9466. Reurtel 9776, November 9, 8 p.m. The Joint Chiefs of Staff and the
                    Department agree to the substitution of the words “may be agreed” for
                    “pre-determines” [“<hi rendition="#italic">he determines</hi>”] in the Protocol
                    on zones of occupation in Germany.<anchor n="57a" xml:id="d199-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d199-f1" n="57a" xml:id="d199-f1-content">
                <p>The Department of Defense has supplied information to the effect that the files
                    of the Joint Chiefs of Staff contain no record of JCS agreement to the
                    substitution referred to in this telegram. The views of the Joint Chiefs of
                    Staff at this time were in general agreement with those of the United States
                    military advisers to the Delegation at the European Advisory Commission, stated
                    in telegram 9776, November 9, from London, <ref target="#pg388">p. 388</ref>,
                    but also included an eventual solution to the problem, in case negotiations
                    became deadlocked, similar to the wording in the final protocol and to that in
                    telegram 9471, November 11, to London, <hi rendition="#italic">infra.</hi>
                </p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
